A||||7||theogre||:CueCat||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||10-01-2000||12:25 AM||||Has anyone gotten one of these yet? Did you read the privacy policy?<P>I'd be at least a tad worried about the security of these people system even if I wasn't worried about what the company itself does with the data.<P>For those that don't know...<P><A HREF="http://www.crq.com/cuecat.html" TARGET=_blank>:CueCat</A> is a bar code reader that hooks in between your keyboard and the computer. It reads several types of bar codes (ISBN UPC and others) and links you to related internet sites.<P>What has many privacy groups peaved is this:<P>1. The bar code wand contains a unique serial number. It transmits all its output in an encoded(encrytped maybe) format that can't be easily read.<P>2. The software requires an activation code. To get the code you must give DigitalConvergence a valid e-mail adress so they can send you the code.<P>3. the registration form asks for additional information that is personally identifiable and that can be used to easly pull up vast amounts of data on a registered user.<P>The biggy is the first 2 can tie the scanner and software to registration data. This allows tracing of every use of the scanner. It has already been established that the basic demographics they ask for can be used to harvest huge volumes of personal data, like how about much money you make, race, and dozens of other things.<P>Digital Convergence has claimed so far that they can't tie personal data to the scanner but basically no one belives it. Even if the scanner serial number is not presently being tied to the registration code it's a simple matter of a software change to do so. The software for the scanner already has an update on the web site.<P>While their privacy policy is pretty it's like most ohters. Subject to change w/o notice and no way to enforce it as it exists.<P>Below is a sample of the data transmitted by just the scanner. The scanner data fields are seperated by periods. The first part of the encrypted reader output is the serial number, notice it doesn't change. the second field is unknown, likely the type of data scanned and control information. the third part appears to be the encrypted content of the bar code label scanned.<P>type/real content/reader sent<P>upc/03952-68594/.C3nZC3nZC3nXCNbXD3j2CxnX.cHCf.E3zXCxb3.<BR>upc/03952-68594/.C3nZC3nZC3nXCNbXD3j2CxnX.fHmc.ChnWENzXDxT2END3.<P>upc/0043-0121-08/.C3nZC3nZC3nXCNbXD3j2CxnX.fHmc.ChnZD3bZCNfYC3T1.<P>upc/34000-44978/.C3nZC3nZC3nXCNbXD3j2CxnX.fHmc.C3b3C3nZD3D6DhTY.<P>upc/24504-01613/.C3nZC3nZC3nXCNbXD3j2CxnX.fHmc.Dhf3DNn3C3j1CNb0.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||09:08 AM||||Ogre--I think I understand how it works (there's something else out there--a camera of sorts--that reads a signature in certain pics in mags), but how many sites could you get to with this?  I can't picture more than a few who would have the correct info (bar code) in their ads.  I'm not interested as I have no problems typing in URLs.  (I guess 3 years of typing in HS has some advantages <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  Just out of curiousity, are you suggesting this, or warning us of it?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-01-2000||12:18 PM||||doesn't just work for things with the "special" cue codes in them. It reads UPC and ISBN code to. You can scan almost anything in and see if it has a link. There are a couple ways they can use that info. They can either use the UPC or ISBN to take you to the makers site or they can tie promo's to it as well. Depends on the deal, if any, they have with the maker of the scanned product.<P>The problem I have with this aspect is most people are going to scan everything in site. This gives CueCat a hell of allot of information. how?<P>UPC codes are assigned kind of like TCP/IP adress ranges are. The first half of the number is assigned by a clearing house. The seocnd half is the product stock #.<P>It is posible to use the first half of the number to target things. For example if you scan lots of purina products they can guess you have pets and target marketing on that. They don't need a deal with purina, the target marketing could be from a competitor.<P>The basic idea isn't bad. It's the fact they can tie the scanner to a name I don't like. (I also don't like that they are encrypting data on my system.) There are allot of ways that information used by this scanner can be abused. If not by Digital Convergence as a company then by people breaking into the system or employee theft. ||||reg||1
