Z||000004||Seanh||10-03-2000||06:04 PM||||I know thats what they do. Iv scanned several Dr. RX's on there, and I get alot of information about them on the web, but I have already been recieving emails about so-call replacment drugs, that do the same things as RX's and are so much cheaper and eaiser to get. I got an email from the Company saying that they had a brake-in and some email addresses were stolen. To make me feel better, I can get a $10 gift certificate for Radio Shack. How thoughtful<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||10-03-2000||06:39 PM||||I just found this on cnn.com:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cnn.com/2000/TECH/computing/10/02/cuecats.ap/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cnn.com/2000/TECH/computing/10/02/cuecats.ap/index.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-03-2000||09:11 PM||||CNET had an article to I think. Last week some time.<P>If they care as little as they say about personal data. Why do you have to give them a valid address so they can e-mail you a second UID to unlock the software?<P>You got to love these people. "We aren't compling data that way." Yet. It's not hard to alter a database and the scanner driver software to tie the two UID's. You know there will be a number of updates to that software. It's not rocket science to make the changes and sucker users to download it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-09-2000||06:40 PM||||If you want to have some fun reading, and find out what else can be done with the dimwit cat the go to hotbot.com and do a quict search for "cuecat"<P>There's all kinds of things going on with these. There's a little software tool called quedog that will tell you everything the scanner outputs. There's also a whole bunch of Perl and Java based stuff for them.||||reg||1
